	Hydrostatic Pressure and Peripheral Arterial Disease (PAD) Diagnostics
The dependent position of the ankle introduces a unique physiological variable absent at the wrist: significant fluctuations in hydrostatic pressure. When a human transitions from a supine to a standing posture, the hydrostatic column of blood exerts increased pressure on the lower extremity vasculature (approximately 80 mmHg at the mid-calf for an average adult), leading to venous blood pooling and a reflex arteriolar vasoconstriction known as the venoarteriolar reflex16. Monitoring the continuous PPG signal at the ankle allows researchers to observe these microvascular adaptations to orthostatic stress, providing a direct window into the autonomic regulation of peripheral vascular tone.
More profoundly, the ankle is the primary anatomical site for the diagnosis and continuous monitoring of Peripheral Arterial Disease (PAD), a systemic manifestation of atherosclerosis characterized by arterial stenosis in the lower limbs. The clinical gold standard for PAD screening is the Ankle-Brachial Index (ABI), which compares the systolic blood pressure at the ankle to that of the brachial artery19. Standard ABI measurements, however, exhibit reduced sensitivity and frequent false negatives in patients with medial arterial calcification—a common pathology in advanced diabetes and chronic kidney disease—because the calcified tibial arteries become mechanically non-compressible under a blood pressure cuff20.
Integrating ankle-worn PPG with traditional hemodynamic assessments resolves this critical diagnostic limitation. The amplitude of the PPG signal (PPGamp) and the ratio of pulsatile to non-pulsatile components (PPGratio) at the posterior tibial artery demonstrate exceptional sensitivity in detecting PAD, effectively bypassing the issue of arterial incompressibility. Clinical evidence indicates that when examining below-knee levels with single-artery stenosis, traditional ABI yields a markedly low diagnostic sensitivity of 19.1%. In stark contrast, the PPGratio derived from an ankle sensor achieves a sensitivity of 68.1%, and PPGamp achieves 55.3%19. The capacity to continuously and non-invasively monitor lower extremity perfusion via an ankle wearable represents a massive physiological advantage for the early detection, prognosis, and longitudinal management of peripheral vascular pathologies19.
Kinematic Superiority: Precision in Step Quantification and Gait Speed
The fundamental purpose of an inertial activity tracker is to quantify human locomotion. The wrist-worn activity tracker inherently relies on a biomechanical assumption: that the rhythmic swing of the arm correlates directly and proportionately with the steps taken by the lower limbs. While this heuristic holds true for healthy adults engaged in vigorous, unimpeded running, it collapses entirely under the realities of free-living conditions, pathological gait, and standard activities of daily living (ADLs).
Eradicating Upper-Limb Artifacts and False Positives
Wrist-worn devices are notoriously vulnerable to false positive step detection. The upper extremities perform a vast array of high-acceleration, non-ambulatory movements. Activities such as washing dishes, folding laundry, playing cards, and brushing teeth generate rhythmic accelerations that consumer wristband algorithms frequently misclassify as locomotion21. Research demonstrates that the Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE) for wrist-worn devices during sedentary, hand-heavy household tasks can exceed 57%21.
Conversely, when the upper limbs are constrained during actual walking—such as when pushing a stroller, carrying a heavy box, or leaning on a shopping cart or walking aid—the wrist tracker fails to detect the dampened arm swing. This results in severe step undercounting, with error rates soaring above 67% when the user relies on a walker or cane21.
By relocating the sensor to the ankle, the IMU is directly coupled to the prime movers of locomotion. The ankle experiences massive, unmistakable acceleration transients during the heel-strike phase of the gait cycle, followed by a distinct deceleration during midstance, and a secondary acceleration peak during toe-off24. These kinematic signatures are virtually impossible to replicate through sedentary activities, ensuring that step counts derived from ankle monitors represent true ambulatory events. Studies explicitly show that ankle-worn accelerometers maintain an error rate approaching 0% during non-ambulatory arm movements, effectively immunizing the dataset against upper-body artifacts21.
Precision in Slow, Pathological, and Geriatric Gait
The algorithmic limitations of wrist accelerometry are most severely pronounced in populations that do not exhibit a vigorous, healthy arm swing. In older adults, the decoupling between arm swing and leg movement causes wrist-worn actigraphy to fail dramatically. Comprehensive studies utilizing the research-grade ActiGraph device demonstrated that for older adults, wrist-worn placement registered a 180% increase in false steps—equivalent to over 5,470 erroneous steps per day—compared to hip or ankle placements26.
For patients recovering from major surgeries or neurological events, the failure mode reverses: wrist trackers drastically undercount steps because these patients walk at speeds below the threshold required to trigger the algorithmic heuristic at the wrist. In early post-operative monitoring of orthopedic and abdominal surgery patients, walking speeds frequently drop below 0.6 meters per second (m/s). At these reduced velocities, popular commercial wrist trackers yield unacceptably high MAPEs, failing to register any steps in nearly a third of walking trials27.
Ankle-worn accelerometers demonstrate exceptional resilience to ultra-low gait speeds. Studies evaluating stroke survivors and functionally impaired older adults walking at speeds as low as 0.3 to 0.5 m/s reveal that waist and wrist sensors produce unacceptable errors (often >10%) and experience total algorithmic failures, recording zero steps despite continuous ambulation23. Under identical conditions, accelerometers positioned at the ankle maintain error rates strictly below 5%, successfully distinguishing stance and swing phases even when forward progression is painfully slow28.


